WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.650
Turn your head,

00:00:00.670 --> 00:00:02.210
and the chair leaves your view.

00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:03.533
But not your mind.

00:00:03.553 --> 00:00:04.903
You still know where it is,

00:00:04.923 --> 00:00:06.328
what one step would reveal,

00:00:06.348 --> 00:00:07.803
and how to get back to it.

00:00:07.823 --> 00:00:09.417
That's a spatial state.

00:00:09.437 --> 00:00:11.292
Visual foundation models learn

00:00:11.312 --> 00:00:13.757
from huge amounts of images and video.

00:00:13.777 --> 00:00:15.667
But do they hold a spatial state?

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.852
We put ten frozen models to the test:

00:00:21.872 --> 00:00:24.753
image and video encoders, a 3D model,

00:00:24.773 --> 00:00:26.915
video generators, a world model,

00:00:26.935 --> 00:00:28.950
and a multimodal LLM.

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:30.685
Each model watches a camera move

00:00:30.705 --> 00:00:32.048
through indoor scenes,

00:00:32.068 --> 00:00:34.760
frame by frame, never seeing the future.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:36.150
A small trained probe

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:38.167
then reads out what its features know.

00:00:38.187 --> 00:00:39.747
We ask four questions.

00:00:39.767 --> 00:00:41.285
Can it read the visible scene:

00:00:41.305 --> 00:00:43.187
depth, pose, and objects?

00:00:43.207 --> 00:00:44.504
Does it remember objects

00:00:44.524 --> 00:00:45.907
after they leave the view?

00:00:45.927 --> 00:00:46.584
Can it predict

00:00:46.604 --> 00:00:48.237
what it will see after a move?

00:00:48.257 --> 00:00:49.332
And can it pick a move

00:00:49.352 --> 00:00:51.227
that brings it closer to a goal?

00:00:52.125 --> 00:00:53.490
Here's what we found.

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:55.333
No model wins everywhere.

00:00:55.353 --> 00:00:57.290
Fast3R reads camera pose

00:00:57.310 --> 00:00:59.583
ten times better than DINOv2.

00:00:59.603 --> 00:01:01.068
Yet the best at locating objects

00:01:01.088 --> 00:01:03.618
out of view is VideoMAE,

00:01:03.638 --> 00:01:06.113
despite weaker readout of the visible scene.

00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:09.048
And DINOv2 and Qwen lead future prediction,

00:01:09.068 --> 00:01:11.475
despite weaker object persistence.

00:01:11.495 --> 00:01:12.512
Seeing a scene well

00:01:12.532 --> 00:01:14.190
does not mean remembering it.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:17.290
Where and when you read also matters.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:20.200
In V-JEPA 2, prediction, object identity,

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:21.687
and persistence each peak

00:01:21.707 --> 00:01:22.967
at a different layer.

00:01:22.987 --> 00:01:25.427
And more history is not always better.

00:01:25.447 --> 00:01:27.312
Going from 8 frames to 24

00:01:27.332 --> 00:01:28.862
makes object persistence

00:01:28.882 --> 00:01:30.612
and path integration worse,

00:01:30.632 --> 00:01:32.487
for every model we tested.

00:01:33.375 --> 00:01:36.150
Next, decodable is not the same as used.

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:38.920
For V-JEPA 2, simply copying the last feature

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:41.100
beats a learned one-step predictor.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:43.505
And VideoMAE, the best

00:01:43.525 --> 00:01:45.243
at locating hidden objects,

00:01:45.263 --> 00:01:46.880
shows no reliable benefit

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:48.460
from its visual history.

00:01:49.337 --> 00:01:51.212
V-JEPA 2 can also predict

00:01:51.232 --> 00:01:52.827
future features natively.

00:01:52.847 --> 00:01:54.017
But hand those predictions

00:01:54.037 --> 00:01:56.717
to a depth reader trained on real features,

00:01:56.737 --> 00:02:00.979
and the error jumps from 0.18 to 0.25,

00:02:00.999 --> 00:02:02.392
worse than a layout prior

00:02:02.412 --> 00:02:03.999
that ignores the input.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:05.070
Retrain the reader,

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:07.525
and the error falls to 0.22.

00:02:07.545 --> 00:02:09.075
Some geometry survives,

00:02:09.095 --> 00:02:10.990
but the interface has changed.

00:02:11.885 --> 00:02:13.850
Finally, acting toward a goal.

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:16.425
Picking the better of two candidate moves works,

00:02:16.445 --> 00:02:18.270
and clearly depends on the goal.

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:19.035
But what if the probe

00:02:19.055 --> 00:02:20.818
must output the move itself?

00:02:20.838 --> 00:02:22.918
Swap in a goal from another scene,

00:02:22.938 --> 00:02:24.568
and the predicted direction swings

00:02:24.588 --> 00:02:26.763
by 30 to 46 degrees.

00:02:26.783 --> 00:02:27.883
Yet the correct goal

00:02:27.903 --> 00:02:30.300
does not reliably beat a shuffled one.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:31.305
The needle turns,

00:02:31.325 --> 00:02:32.790
but it doesn't point the way.

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:35.600
Goal sensitivity is not goal utility.

00:02:36.502 --> 00:02:37.857
So readable, used,

00:02:37.877 --> 00:02:40.167
and useful are three different questions.

00:02:40.187 --> 00:02:41.607
To judge whether a vision model

00:02:41.627 --> 00:02:43.107
holds a spatial state,

00:02:43.127 --> 00:02:45.207
don't just ask what can be decoded.

00:02:45.227 --> 00:02:47.332
Ask whether it can be kept beyond view,

00:02:47.352 --> 00:02:49.957
updated under motion, and used to act.
